// endpoint_claim_live_notification.proto — ClaimLiveNotification: Emacs came
// forward while a desktop banner may still be showing; which workspace does
// the newest live one name? HOST section.
//
// Emacs is the only sender. It calls this once the daemon has ACCEPTED its
// report that it gained desktop focus (ReportEditorFocus answered `success`
// for a `focused` report that followed an unfocused one), whatever brought it
// forward: the dock, an application switcher, a click on one of its frames.
// On a `live` answer Emacs selects the named workspace exactly as any other
// switch: it REQUESTS the switch (SelectWorkspace) and its frame follows the
// roster's `current`.
//
// A BANNER IS LIVE while the platform still holds it: on macOS while
// Notification Center still lists it (the banner program's own delivered
// list), on Linux while the banner program still shows it awaiting its click.
// A banner the user clicked or cleared is not live.
//
// A CLAIM CONSUMES. The daemon answers the newest live banner made since the
// last claim it answered or the last workspace selection it served, and then
// forgets every banner it held, live or not: each banner steers at most one
// return to Emacs, and none made before the user last chose a workspace ever
// steers one. A banner's own click is such a selection, so clicking an older
// banner is never overridden by a newer one.
//
